(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2608319900-credential-shape-is-per-apex-and-the-probe-reads-one-shape")
  (:adr/title "The Biscuit shape is per-apex, so a probe that presents one shape reports a false negative — murakumo reads the header it is scored as ignoring")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-31")

  (:adr/context
  "ADR-2608311600 measures six apexes against five invariants and scored
   36.67/100 with 22 findings. One of those findings is wrong, and the way it is
   wrong generalises.

   The axis `plane-reads-capability` (headroom 0.20) asks whether presenting a
   Biscuit changes what a data plane answers. The probe answers it by sending
   one token shape to all six apexes and comparing the bare response with the
   credentialed one. For murakumo.cloud it read no change and recorded that the
   plane does not read the header.

   It does read it. Measured live 2026-08-31 against
   api.murakumo.cloud/v1/chat/completions, three requests, all curl_rc=0:

     bare                       402  external_model_requires_payment
     Biscuit mrb_<100 chars>    401  authentication_error, invalid or
                                     insufficient Murakumo credential
     Biscuit EnwAAA             402  byte-identical to bare

   The middle row is the whole answer: a correctly shaped token reaches
   AUTHN_SERVICE and comes back with a distinct status and body. The third row
   is what the probe sends.

   The gate is one regex, in cloud-murakumo-api src/inference_auth.js line 11 —
   it accepts either the Bearer or the Biscuit scheme, and requires an mrb_
   prefix with 80 to 8192 characters of base64url. A six-character token does
   not match, so the request is never forwarded to AUTHN_SERVICE and falls
   through to the anonymous path, which answers the same 402 as no header at
   all. Correct behaviour, indistinguishable from indifference.")

  (:adr/decision
  "1. murakumo.cloud passes `plane-reads-capability`. Its score on that probe is
      80 only because of the shape mismatch; nothing there needs fixing, and an
      agent sent to fix it would be looking for a defect that is not present.

   2. CREDENTIAL SHAPE IS PER-APEX, and that is by design rather than drift.
      murakumo prefixes mrb_ (ADR-2608291300 established the mro_ operator form
      of the same convention); kotobase accepts a bare base64url biscuit and
      parses it far enough to name a reason. A cross-apex probe must carry a
      shape per apex, exactly as it already carries an auth host per apex.

   3. This ADR does not change the probe. `90-docs/auth_plane_integration/probe.cljs`
      was under uncommitted edit by another session while this was measured, and
      that session is already replacing the six-character token with a
      200-character one and measuring several planes per apex. Two sessions
      editing one instrument is how an instrument stops being trusted.

      Note for whoever lands that work: a 200-character run of the letter A is
      still not an mrb_ token, so the murakumo false negative survives that
      change. The shape has to come from the apex table.")

  (:adr/evidence
  "What was measured, and what was measured badly.

   SOLID — the three requests above. All three returned curl_rc=0, which is
   stated because an earlier attempt in the same session did not: a token built
   through base64 carried a newline, curl exited 43 without reaching the server,
   and the differing output briefly looked like proof that the plane reads the
   header. A failed request and a different answer are not the same evidence,
   and the first version of this measurement confused them.

   SOLID — the regex, read from source rather than inferred from behaviour.

   NOT MEASURED — itonami.cloud. Its four findings were not checked here. The
   endpoint reached for a data-plane comparison answered 502 in both directions,
   so nothing about that apex is claimed. auth.itonami.cloud/v1/biscuit/token
   and its kotobase counterpart both answer 403 to a cross-origin caller, which
   is what the kotobase probe scores as issuance present and guarded — but
   reading that as a pass for itonami would be asserting the answer this ADR
   exists to question.")

  (:adr/consequences
  "The headline number understates the fleet by one axis on one apex. That
   matters less than the shape of the error: a probe that holds no credential
   can only present a syntactically shaped one, and syntax is exactly what
   differs between apexes. Every axis of that kind — present something, see
   whether the answer moves — carries the same risk wherever the presented
   thing has a per-apex form.

   The reverse risk is worth naming too. Had murakumo answered 401 to the
   six-character token, the probe would have scored a pass, and the pass would
   have said nothing about whether a real Biscuit is honoured. The axis
   discriminates only when the token is shaped well enough to reach the
   verifier and still be rejected by it.

   Not decided here: whether the apex table should carry a token shape, or
   whether each apex should accept a common shape. The first is a change to an
   instrument; the second is a change to four deployed gates.")

  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608311600-one-auth-design-across-six-apexes"
                "adr-2608291700-passkey-and-smart-contract-authenticate-biscuit-authorizes"
                "adr-2608291300-passkey-rooted-operator-authority-on-api-murakumo-cloud"))))
